2Z","branch":"master","commit_sha":"b1da59351bb15c6e33425de78ca76ab51add047c","github_link":"https://github.com/pyushkevich/itksnap/commit/b1da59351bb15c6e33425de78ca76ab51add047c","commit_subject":"Fix the first 4D CTA time point loading upside down (#128)","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"vpnhood/VpnHood","commit_timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:19:01Z","branch":"develop","commit_sha":"54b763cd7551021f362281ef493b32efe9f45e1d","github_link":"https://github.com/vpnhood/VpnHood/commit/54b763cd7551021f362281ef493b32efe9f45e1d","commit_subject":"Linux: NetworkManager is kept off the VPN's tun. It adopted the tun as connected externally, so a desktop's network tray announced every connect and disconnect - on Lubuntu, nm-tray's \"Connection established: tun 'VpnHoodClient'\", then \"Connection lost: No longer connected to 802-3-ethernet ''\", as if the Ethernet had gone. Before it makes the tun, the adapter writes /run/udev/rules.d/90-<adapter>.rules, which marks the name NM_UNMANAGED, and reloads udev's rules (ExcludeFromNetworkManager); NetworkManager waits for udev before it looks at a device, so it never takes the tun. Once per boot, as /run is emptied at boot; a failure is a warning, since the VPN works the same without the rule; skipped where udev does not run. Tested on Lubuntu 26.04 (LXQt, nm-tray) with this build of the adapter in the 9.1.867 prerelease, which differs from it in this file only: the tun unmanaged, no notice at connect or disconnect, DNS on the tun, the public IP the server's, a reconnect reusing the rule; a tun made by hand without the rule still raised both notices. With its CHANGELOG line.","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"commonfabric/labs","commit_timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:18:48Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"4d0cef54c4a3246f83984d288ece11cf8392afba","github_link":"https://github.com/commonfabric/labs/commit/4d0cef54c4a3246f83984d288ece11cf8392afba","commit_subject":"fix(piece): a pattern update may stop publishing an optional result field (#955